C0 line 245: forbidden transfer from "no proper future C2 extension" to "this class" (C0) with reason "C2 is a strictly larger extension class, so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker". But E_C2 is the SMALLEST extension set, not larger. The reason's premise token is inverted: C2 is a strictly smaller extension class (set of extensions). The conclusion (transfer forbidden) is correct: C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker than C0-inextendibility. So H1: the reason text is wrong (inverted premise), while the conclusion is right. My prior verdict said exactly this.

Now C2's own line 242: transfer from "no proper future C2 extension" to "no proper future C0 extension" — forbidden, reason "the converse containment is false". Let's check: The claim "no C2 ext => no C0 ext" is FALSE (as established). The reason "the converse containment is false" — what does that mean? The transfer rule presumably would be licensed by containment E_C0 ⊆ E_C2 (if every C0 extension were C2, then no C2 ext would imply no C0 ext). The stated reason "the converse containment is false" is ambiguous. Which containment is "the converse"? The needed containment is E_C0 ⊆ E_C2; the actual containment is E_C2 ⊆ E_C0. So "the converse containment is false" is a correct (if terse) justification: the containment that would license the transfer (converse of the actual one) is false. OK, that's fine but the phrase "converse containment is false" is vague, and could be judged as an inverted-premise style defect... Actually no: it says the converse containment is false, which correctly denies the licensing containment. Given actual E_C2 ⊆ E_C0, the "converse" E_C0 ⊆ E_C2 is indeed false. Fine.

Line 243: transfer C2→H2loc forbidden, reason "the converse containment is false; C2-inextendibility is weaker than H2_loc-inextendibility". E_C2 ⊆ E_H2loc; C2-inext (E_C2=∅) does not imply H2loc-inext (E_H2loc=∅); indeed C2-inext is weaker. Correct.

Now compare C0 line 245 reason: "C2 is a strictly larger extension class, so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker". If "extension class" means the set of extensions, C2 is smaller (E_C2 ⊆ E_C0). If "extension class" means regularity strength, C2 is a stronger/higher regularity. The phrase "larger extension class" is ambiguous but the document itself defines "extension_class_containment: E_C0 contains E_H2loc contains E_{C^1,1} contains E_C2" — so "extension class" = E_X set, and C2 is the smallest. So the C0 reason token is inverted/misleading.

